course: dictionary
lesson: 8073
title: Relieve: more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and context tells you which one to use. Relieve means to make pain, worry or a problem less. «The medicine relieved his pain.» The medicine made the pain less, so relieve means to ease it.
explain: The same word can also mean to take over someone's job so they can rest. «The night guard came to relieve the day guard.» The clue is the job: one guard arrives to take over from the other, like a change of shift. Ask yourself which meaning fits the clues around the word.
explain: Sometimes relieve is formal. «He was relieved of his duties» means he was removed from his job. «Let go», «shown the door» and «laid off» also mean lost a job, but they sound like everyday talk, while «relieved of his duties» sounds official.
fact: Relieve means to make pain, worry or a problem less, or to take it away.
fact: Relieve can also mean to take over someone's job or duty, so they can rest.
fact: «Relieved of his duties» is a formal way to say he lost his job.
fact: «Relieve yourself» is a polite, euphemism way to say go to the toilet.
fact: Phrases you may meet are relieve pain, relieve stress and relieved to hear, which means glad that a worry was not true.
exercise: «The medicine relieved his headache.» What does relieve mean here? | *To make the pain less | To forget about it | To paint over it | To make the headache bigger
exercise: «The night guard came to relieve the day guard.» What does relieve mean? | To give them a gift | *To take over the job so they can rest | To shout at them | To lock the door
exercise: «He was relieved of his duties after the mistake.» What does this mean? | He was given a medal | He had a long rest | *He lost his job | He went on a long holiday
test: «She felt relieved to hear the test was cancelled.» What does relieved suggest? | Tired of the school bus | Angry about the cancelled test | *Happy that a worry had gone | Hungry for lunch
test: Ben says «shown the door» is posh, so it must be the official phrase. Is he right? | *No, it is everyday talk, while «relieved of his duties» is official | No, because it means to open a door | Yes, because it means relieved of pain | Yes, it is the most formal phrase of all
exam: Which phrase is the formal way to say someone lost their job? | relieve pain | relieve stress | relieved to hear | *relieved of his duties
